We formalize evidence-grounded DocRE as structured generation and compare Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), Direct Preference Optimization (DPO), and Rejection Sampling Fine-Tuning (RSFT) across three fully evaluated scales (1.7B, 4B, 8B) with 14B validation, two model families, and two datasets. The evidence tax---extraction cost of generating evidence---is scale-dependent: beneficial at 1.7B ($-$5.63pp dev Ign-F1) but approximately neutral at $\geq$4B (+0.25$\pm$0.47pp at 4B; positive $=$ cost). Gradient-based optimization amplifies evidence-extraction competition (GRPO catastrophic, DPO gradual), while RSFT with Natural Language Inference (NLI)-based scoring avoids decoupling entirely through output filtering rather than gradient pressure.
RSFT consistently reduces training seed variance (complete pairwise seed separation on LLaMA, Cohen's $d{=}2.58$; non-overlapping $\chi^2$ confidence intervals at 1.7B). Our 1.7B RSFT model (51.19 Ign-F1, std 0.09pp) approaches 8B Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) performance (50.19, std 3.23pp) at 4.7$\times$ fewer parameters. Test-set evaluation preserves model ranking across all evaluated scales (Spearman $\rho{=}1.0$).
\end{abstract}

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We formalize evidence-grounded document-level relation extraction as a structured generation task and compare three post-training paradigms across three fully evaluated scales (1.7B, 4B, 8B) with 14B validation, two model families, and two datasets. The evidence tax is not a fixed penalty but a landscape shaped by capacity, optimization paradigm, and data quality filtering.
RSFT improves mean extraction across all tested scales and reduces training variance at every evaluated configuration; a 1.7B RSFT model approaches 8B SFT performance at 4.7$\times$ fewer parameters.
Future work should investigate targeted evidence rewards and extend evaluation to non-Wikipedia domains.

